# ===========================================================================
# DEPLOYMENT PROFILE  (amendment 2026-08-28 -- REQUIRED, no default)
# ===========================================================================
#
# Decides which LLM provider is live and which capability qualifications may be
# reused. It is DECLARED, never inferred from a URL, a hostname, an API key or a
# model name -- inference is how a machine ends up believing it is somewhere it
# is not. An unset or unknown value stops the process at import.
#
#   local   the official OpenAI API              <- development
#   aramco  the internal OpenAI-compatible gateway  (DEFERRED, and blocked until
#           a capability test succeeds inside that network)
#   test    an in-process stub; fixtures only
#
# EACH ENVIRONMENT IS QUALIFIED INDEPENDENTLY. A successful `local` capability
# artifact can never qualify `aramco` -- deployment_profile is one of the
# identity fields compared at admission.
DEPLOYMENT_PROFILE=local

# --- local profile: the official OpenAI API --------------------------------
#
# REAL SECRETS BELONG ONLY IN backend/.env, WHICH IS NEVER COMMITTED.
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Optional account scoping. Identifiers, not secrets, but still deployment-
# specific and never hard-coded.
# OPENAI_ORG_ID=
# OPENAI_PROJECT_ID=

# The SELECTED local verifier model (owner decision, 2026-08-29 --
# docs/decisions/2026-08-29-verifier-model-selection.md). This is the default, so
# the line stays commented; set it explicitly if you want the choice declared in
# the file rather than inherited from code.
#
# It is the THIRD explicitly selected model for this profile. The two before it
# were each MEASURED AND FOUND INELIGIBLE, for different reasons, and neither
# reason was structured output:
#
#   gpt-4o-2024-08-06                    probes 1-6 pass; probe 7 measured a 0.25
#                                        malformed-response rate -- spec 15.1
#                                        case C returned per-requirement statuses
#                                        contradicting its own declared outcome,
#                                        which check 15 rejects
#   gpt-5-mini-2025-08-07, effort unset  33/33 calls valid, zero operational,
#                                        validation or consistency failures; spec
#                                        15.1 case E unstable across three repeats
#   gpt-5-mini-2025-08-07, effort=high   case-E diagnostic passed 0 of 5
#
# Every one of those records stays on disk, stays accurate about what it
# measured, and qualifies nothing.
#
# NOTHING WAS WEAKENED TO GET HERE. Case E was reviewed and is correctly
# specified -- the byte-pinned system prompt states that a passage depending on an
# unsupplied referenced figure is an UNCLEAR case -- so the expectation stands and
# the model was wrong. No case, schema, prompt or check was changed to fit a
# measurement, and there is NO automatic fallback and NO runtime model-selection
# chain: three selections in a row is three owner decisions, not a search loop.
#
# Pinned to a SNAPSHOT rather than a floating alias. A capability artifact records
# the revision it qualified, and an alias can be moved to a new snapshot by the
# provider without a single line of configuration changing -- which would leave
# the artifact matching on every field while describing a model that no longer
# exists. Changing this re-runs the capability gate; an artifact never transfers
# across models.
#
# NOTE the request shape follows the model: `deployment.MODEL_DIALECTS` sends
# `max_completion_tokens` and omits `temperature` for the reasoning families,
# because they reject the classic names outright. EVERY gpt-5.x version has its
# OWN row there and none of them is gpt-5 under a longer name: gpt-5.1 removed
# `minimal` and added `none`, and gpt-5.4-mini keeps `none` and adds `xhigh`.
# Matching is at a VERSION BOUNDARY, so `gpt-5` cannot annex `gpt-5.4-mini` --
# without that, a selected model runs under a neighbour's vocabulary, allowance
# and timeout floor and nothing fails. A model whose dialect is unlisted gets the
# classic shape and fails loudly if that is wrong, which is the signal to add a
# row rather than to retry differently.
#
# Owner decision 2026-08-31: gpt-5.4-mini-2026-03-17, pinned to a snapshot and
# NOT the floating `gpt-5.4-mini` alias -- an alias can be moved by the provider
# with no configuration change, leaving an artifact that matches on every field
# while describing a model that no longer exists.
# OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-5.4-mini-2026-03-17

# Reasoning effort, sent as the provider's own `reasoning_effort` parameter.
# Owner decision 2026-08-31: `medium` on gpt-5.4-mini-2026-03-17.
#
# The history is kept because it is the reason this setting is recorded at all:
# `high` was chosen on 2026-08-29 after gpt-5-mini was measured ineligible at the
# provider's default effort -- spec 15.1 case E (the conservative-ambiguity case)
# returned UNCLEAR, PARTIALLY, PARTIALLY across three repeats -- and carried
# forward to gpt-5.1-2025-11-13, which was then also measured ineligible.
#
# Allowed values are PER MODEL FAMILY, not global:
#   gpt-5.4-mini      none | low | medium | high | xhigh
#   gpt-5.1           none | low | medium | high
#   gpt-5 / o1/o3/o4  minimal | low | medium | high
# Unset leaves the provider's own default in force, which is itself a recorded
# value in the identity rather than a gap.
#
# A value no family accepts raises ConfigError; a value THIS model's family does
# not accept also raises, BEFORE any paid call and before the request is even
# built. It is never dropped and never downgraded to a neighbouring value,
# because a dropped setting runs at an effort the operator does not believe is in
# force and the artifact would then describe a runtime nobody tested.
#
# CHANGING THIS RE-RUNS THE CAPABILITY GATE. It is part of
# `inference_config_fingerprint`, which is one of the identity fields admission
# compares -- an artifact measured at one effort cannot qualify another, in
# either direction.
OPENAI_REASONING_EFFORT=medium

# Override only to reach a compatible proxy. A different endpoint is a DIFFERENT
# RUNTIME and needs its own capability artifact -- a proxy can rewrite a request,
# strip response_format, or serve a different revision.
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1

# --- aramco profile: the internal gateway (DEFERRED) -----------------------
#
# Read only when DEPLOYMENT_PROFILE=aramco. Left configured so the profile is
# ready; it is blocked until `python -m verification.capability_test` succeeds
# from inside that network and produces an `aramco` artifact.
# LLM_API_URL=...
# LLM_MODEL=...
# LLM_API_KEY=...

# --- embeddings are SEPARATE and did NOT move ------------------------------
#
# The Hugging Face embedding path stays exactly as approved. LLM provider
# configuration and embedding provider configuration are different settings and
# are changed separately -- see EMBEDDING_API_URL / EMBEDDING_MODEL /
# EMBEDDING_API_KEY below. Qdrant likewise stays in its documented state; a
# change of LLM provider is not a reason to enable semantic search.

# Hugging Face inference token.
# Required. Backs both the LLM calls (llm.py) and the embedding calls
# (embeddings.py) - there is no separate embedding credential.
HF_TOKEN=your_huggingface_token_here

# SerpApi key, used for the Google Patents search source.
# Required: Google Patents is currently the only enabled provider.
# https://serpapi.com/
SERPAPI_KEY=your_serpapi_key_here

# EPO Open Patent Services (OPS) API credentials.
# Currently unused - the EPO provider is commented out of SOURCES in
# backend/search/search.py while its OPS auth is being throttled. Keep these set
# so re-enabling it is a one-line change.
# https://developers.epo.org/
EPO_CONSUMER_KEY=your_epo_ops_consumer_key_here
EPO_CONSUMER_SECRET=your_epo_ops_consumer_secret_here

# OpenAI-compatible /v1/embeddings endpoint backing EMBEDDING_MODEL.
# Required: embeddings.py speaks the OpenAI request/response shape only, so
# there is no default to fall back to. Ranking calls it on every request.
EMBEDDING_API_URL=https://your-gateway.example.com/v1/embeddings

# Lens.org Patent API bearer token, created from the Subscriptions tab of a
# Lens user profile (lens.org/lens/user/subscriptions/requested). Currently
# unused - the Lens provider is commented out of SOURCES in
# backend/search/search.py because this is a 14-day non-commercial trial
# token, not a subscription. Trial use is for evaluation only - see
# https://about.lens.org/lens-api-terms-of-use/ before enabling it beyond
# local testing. Continued/commercial use requires contacting
# subscriptions@lens.org for a paid plan.
# https://docs.api.lens.org/
LENS_API_KEY=your_lens_api_trial_token_here

# Optional overrides - defaults shown below
# LLM_API_URL=https://router.huggingface.co/v1/chat/completions
# LLM_MODEL=meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct:novita
# EMBEDDING_MODEL=BAAI/bge-large-en-v1.5

# Bearer tokens for the two endpoints above. Both optional: LLM_API_KEY falls
# back to HF_TOKEN, and EMBEDDING_API_KEY falls back to LLM_API_KEY (then
# HF_TOKEN), since one gateway commonly serves both with one key. Set either
# separately only when that provider needs its own credential.
# LLM_API_KEY=
# EMBEDDING_API_KEY=

# Qdrant, from the repository-root docker-compose.yml. Required: the graph's
# semantic-search branch reads it and index_patents writes it, with no fallback
# on either side. The default below already matches that compose file; override
# only if you changed the published port or point at a shared instance.
# QDRANT_URL=http://127.0.0.1:6333
# QDRANT_API_KEY=

# PostgreSQL, from the repository-root docker-compose.yml. Required: every
# completed run is stored before its result is sent, and GET /api/history and
# the login lookup both read it, with no fallback on any of the three. The
# default below already matches that compose file; override only if you changed
# the published port or point at a shared instance.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://agenticai:agenticai@127.0.0.1:5432/agenticai

# HMAC key the session tokens in backend/auth.py are signed with. The default is
# a known constant checked into config.py, so ANY backend that does not set this
# will accept a token minted by anyone who has read this repository. Set it to a
# real random value (e.g. `python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_hex(32))"`)
# before exposing the backend beyond localhost. Changing it invalidates every
# issued token, which is also the only way to revoke one.
# AUTH_SECRET=
# AUTH_SESSION_HOURS=12

# Set TRACE=0 to silence the per-step trace printed to stdout on every request.
# TRACE=0

# Feature-disclosure verification (backend/verification/, specified in
# docs/prompts/feature-disclosure-verification.md).
#
# The verifier requires JSON Schema-constrained generation, which is an
# eligibility requirement rather than a preference: a model or adapter that
# cannot provide it is not eligible to serve the verifier, and the stage fails
# with a capability error instead of degrading. Leave this alone unless you have
# run the specification's section 14.2.1 capability test.
# VERIFIER_STRUCTURED_OUTPUT=json_schema
#
# Setting it to `json_object` asks the verifier to run with generic JSON mode -
# syntactically valid JSON and nothing more: no required fields, no closed enums,
# no rejection of additional properties. The specification permits that as an
# explicit MVP decision, never as a silent runtime downgrade, so it also requires
# the decision to be written down here: the owner, the date, the model and
# adapter revisions it covers, and the measured malformed-response rate. An empty
# value with `json_object` selected is a capability error, not a warning.
# VERIFIER_GENERIC_JSON_DECISION=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Durable workflow execution (backend/workflow/)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Verified runs pause for human review measured in minutes to days, so they
# cannot be driven by an HTTP request the way POST /api/analyze/stream is. A
# separate, database-driven executor drives them; these settings configure the
# process that hosts it and the lease that makes at most one worker the
# authorized writer at a time.
#
# None of this affects POST /api/analyze or POST /api/analyze/stream, which keep
# their exact current behaviour: inline execution, no run row, no lease, no event
# log.

# Where the executor loop runs. `worker` is the production topology: a SECOND
# WORKLOAD, `python -m workflow.worker`, alongside the API. `inprocess` runs the
# identical loop on a daemon thread inside the API for local development - still
# lease-protected, still database-driven, still recovering after a restart -
# started by server.py's lifespan and stopped cleanly at shutdown.
#
# This is not the deleted PIPELINE flag pattern: that flag chose between two
# parallel implementations, this chooses where ONE implementation runs.
#
# An unrecognised value FAILS API STARTUP, and so does `inprocess` with no
# WORKFLOW_GRAPH_ADAPTER below. There is no mock fallback on either path: quietly
# substituting the deterministic adapter would let a deployment believe it was
# running real verification while it fabricated everything.
# WORKFLOW_EXECUTOR=worker

# `module:attribute` of the workflow.executor.GraphAdapter the executor drives.
# Deliberately empty by default and deliberately without a fallback: an executor
# with no graph refuses to start rather than claiming runs it cannot advance, and
# a default would silently run the wrong graph. There is no verification graph
# yet - `python -m workflow.worker --mock` selects the built-in deterministic
# adapter, which makes no outbound HTTP calls at all.
# WORKFLOW_GRAPH_ADAPTER=

# The lease. LEASE_HEARTBEAT_SECONDS must be STRICTLY BELOW LEASE_TTL_SECONDS:
# at or above it the lease lapses between beats and the worker fences itself
# mid-run, which presents as a run that mysteriously stalls and is reclaimed by a
# replacement. Both must be positive.
#
# An invalid value STOPS THE WORKER FROM STARTING. It is never silently replaced
# with a default, because a default that quietly overrides a deliberate setting
# is how a misconfiguration survives a deploy unnoticed. The SQL functions
# validate the TTL again at their own boundary, because a function is not
# entitled to trust its caller.
# LEASE_TTL_SECONDS=60
# LEASE_HEARTBEAT_SECONDS=20

# How often the executor polls for a claimable run when it found none.
# WORKFLOW_POLL_INTERVAL_SECONDS=2.0

# How often an SSE reader polls workflow_events for new rows. Correctness is
# polling; LISTEN/NOTIFY may only ever be a latency optimisation on top of it,
# because the API pod streaming a run is usually not the worker executing it.
# WORKFLOW_EVENT_POLL_MS=500

# Backpressure. A client that cannot keep up is DISCONNECTED, never buffered
# unboundedly - nothing is dropped, because every frame is still in
# workflow_events and the client reconnects with its last id. A slow or vanished
# client never stalls, cancels, pauses or fails the run.
# SSE_CLIENT_BUFFER=256
# SSE_WRITE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30

# Retention for `python -m workflow.cleanup`. It removes only `progress` and
# `verification_progress` rows from finished runs older than this and raises the
# replay watermark; lifecycle events - review_required, paused, resumed, and the
# terminal - are kept for as long as the run row exists, whatever this says.
# WORKFLOW_EVENT_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Evidence layer: date screening and text version (Implementation Gate B)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# These encode rules with LEGAL consequences, so NEITHER HAS A DEFAULT and
# neither may be chosen by whoever writes the code. Admission refuses a run whose
# policy is unset or whose decision is unrecorded - the same shape as
# VERIFIER_GENERIC_JSON_DECISION above: one pattern for "a decision a human must
# record before the machine may act".
#
# The decisions below were put to the repository owner and ANSWERED ON 2026-08-28,
# in the request that authorized Implementation Gate B's completion. They were not
# approved earlier. Uncomment them to enable date-screened runs.
#
# Owner, rationale, revisit conditions and the measured EPO coverage limitation
# they were accepted alongside:  docs/decisions/2026-08-28-evidence-layer-policies.md

# Which date screens prior art, and where the cutoff edge sits.
#
#   publication_lt_cutoff   the evaluated publication's OWN publication date,
#                           STRICTLY BEFORE the cutoff   <- APPROVED
#   publication_lte_cutoff  the same date, on or before the cutoff
#
# The priority date is stored and displayed beside it, and NEVER screens.
# DATE_SCREENING_POLICY=publication_lt_cutoff

# Who approved that, when, and why. Free text - a config file cannot check any of
# it, but it CAN refuse to run until somebody writes it down.
# DATE_SCREENING_DECISION=Approved 2026-08-28 by the repository owner: the evaluated publication's own publication date, exclusive of the cutoff. An uncertain date is EXCLUDED_DATE with certainty UNCERTAIN, surfaced at Workflow Gate B, never silently passed.

# What a passage's text_version_date may be when EPO supplies no document
# version date.
#
#   provider_publication_date  the served document's publication date from EPO
#                              biblio, recorded as PROVIDER_PUBLICATION_DATE.
#                              Provider metadata, honest that it identifies the
#                              version only as coarsely as the publication does.
#                              <- APPROVED
#   exclude                    no trustworthy version means no passage:
#                              EXCLUDED_INELIGIBLE for that publication.
#
# RETRIEVAL TIME IS NEVER AN OPTION. retrieved_at says when we fetched, not which
# version we fetched, and substituting it would date a stored verdict against a
# document version nobody can identify.
# TEXT_VERSION_POLICY=provider_publication_date

# TEXT_VERSION_DECISION=Approved 2026-08-28 by the repository owner: the served document's publication date from EPO bibliographic data. retrieved_at is stored separately and is never substituted.

# A BIBLIOGRAPHIC REQUEST FAILURE IS NOT AN ABSENT DATE, under either policy. A
# timeout, 401, 403, 404, 429, 5xx or malformed XML FAILS THE RUN - it never
# becomes an exclusion and never becomes NO_TEXT. Converting a provider outage
# into "this publication is ineligible" reports a fact nobody established and
# understates coverage using the provider's own downtime.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EPO-only enrichment: accepted, temporarily, with a measured cost
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Not a setting - a recorded decision, kept here because it governs what the runs
# configured above actually cover. Approved 2026-08-28 by the repository owner.
#
# Measured on 34 stored publications that same day
# (docs/measurements/epo-coverage-2026-08-28.md):
#
#   overall coverage        17.6%  (6 of 34)
#   would be excluded       82.4%  (28 of 34) as EXCLUDED_NO_FULL_TEXT
#   ZERO coverage           US 0/18, KR 0/6, CN 0/2, DK 0/1
#   coverage                EP 75%, CA 100%, WO 100%
#
# Those offices are not under-served, they are excluded entirely: EPO's full-text
# service holds EP/WO-family documents.
#
# Accepting this obliges the product and the report to say so. Every exclusion is
# visible and reasoned; excluded publications are stated to have NOT BEEN
# EVALUATED for feature disclosure; the result is never described as a complete or
# exhaustive novelty search; and no claim that no prior art exists is ever made on
# the basis that EPO lacked full text.
#
# A SECOND ENRICHMENT SOURCE IS A POST-MVP PRIORITY. Discovery is NOT narrowed to
# EPO's coverage as part of this work.

# --- verifier capability enforcement ---------------------------------------
#
# Whether a verified run REQUIRES an eligible spec 14.2.1 capability artifact.
#
#   required  (default) the specification's position. No artifact, no match, or a
#             matching-but-INELIGIBLE artifact all refuse admission.
#   deferred  admit the run anyway, RECORDING that this runtime is not qualified.
#
# `deferred` is NOT a bypass, and the difference is the whole point:
#
#   * a bypass proceeds AS THOUGH qualified, and nothing downstream can tell;
#   * a deferral proceeds while recording that it is not, returns
#     `capability_qualified: false` from POST /api/runs, carries that into the
#     report as a stated limitation, and STILL runs all eighteen section 9 checks
#     on every response.
#
# Nothing about validation is relaxed. A model whose output does not conform
# fails the cell with its failing check named and the run keeps its state, which
# is exactly why deferring is survivable.
#
# Owner decision 2026-08-29: local development defers, because paid API spending
# is reserved for real searches a user starts through the product. This is NOT
# evidence that the configured model is eligible. gpt-5.4-mini-2026-03-17 is
# SELECTED AND UNMEASURED -- no run has been made against it, which is a
# different state from the measured ineligibility of gpt-4o, gpt-5-mini and
# gpt-5.1, and neither state qualifies anything. See
# docs/measurements/structured-output-capability-STATUS.md.
#
# An unknown value raises at admission rather than being read as either one.
# VERIFIER_CAPABILITY_ENFORCEMENT=required

# Required whenever the above is `deferred`: who deferred, when, and why. A
# policy chosen with nobody recorded as having chosen it is indistinguishable
# from a default nobody noticed.
# VERIFIER_CAPABILITY_DEFERRAL_DECISION=

# --- Owner decision 2026-08-30: the advanced-workflow redesign -------------
#
# Development and acceptance of the redesigned advanced workflow run DEFERRED,
# because no runtime has qualified and building the four inventor-facing stages
# against a gate that refuses every run would test nothing. Uncomment BOTH lines
# together -- `deferred` without a recorded decision refuses at admission, which
# is the point.
#
# **This is a deferral, not a bypass, and production release stays blocked.**
# Admission returns `capability_qualified: false`, every one of the eighteen
# section 9 checks still runs on every response, no eligible artifact was
# fabricated, and `require_capability` was not weakened. A run admitted this way
# produces verdicts from a verifier this runtime has never qualified, and the
# person starting it is told so.
#
# VERIFIER_CAPABILITY_ENFORCEMENT=deferred
# VERIFIER_CAPABILITY_DEFERRAL_DECISION=Approved 2026-08-30 by the repository owner: development and acceptance of the redesigned advanced workflow proceed with the verifier unqualified. Four runtimes have been measured ineligible (see docs/measurements/structured-output-capability-STATUS.md). Production release remains blocked until a runtime is formally qualified.
